  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nOutflow from a coastal low pressure system situated southeast of\nthe Carolinas will continue to advect into the region from the\nover the next 24 hours, prompting an increase in high level \nclouds. With persistent southerly flow and high pressure \nremaining in place, temperatures today into tomorrow will\ncontinue to run above seasonal averages. \n\nAs the coastal low tracks east of Delaware and new New Jersey\nSunday afternoon, precipitation chances may gradually increase\nnear the Laurel Highlands and West Virginia ridges. However, dry\nair in the mid and lower levels of the atmosphere may limit\nprecip development or prevent it from reaching the ground due to\nevaporation. \n\nThe ridge of high pressure will gradually break down Sunday\nnight into Monday as a cold front advances east from the Midwest\ninto the Ohio River Valley. Falling temperatures and periods of\nlight rain in the vicinity of the front are expected before\ndaybreak Monday. A brief lull in precipitation is anticipated in\nthe wake of the frontal passage before northwest flow develops,\nbringing spotty lake-enhanced rain and perhaps a mix of snow to\nareas north of Pittsburgh late Monday into Tuesday. However, a \nrelatively weak fetch off the Great Lakes will limit the \nduration and intensity of lake enhancement, with shower coverage\ngradually diminishing Tuesday afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nSeveral long-range models indicate the development of a\ndisturbance near the Mississippi Delta early Wednesday as an \nupper-level trough shifts eastward out of the Rockies. By early \nThursday morning, models suggest the development of a new \ncoastal low near the Carolinas, which is expected to track north\ntowards New England. Given the anticipated southerly flow ahead\nof the system as it approaches the region, precipitation type \ntrends continue to favor rain, with the probability of advisory-\nlevel winter weather remaining low, even across the ridges. \n\nIn a colder, lower-probability scenario, snowfall accumulations\nof 1-2 inches could occur along the Laurel Highlands and West\nVirginia ridges. Current ensemble guidance shows a broad spread\nbetween the 25th and 75th percentiles, ranging from little to no\nmeasurable precip (the coastal low located more-so out to sea) \nto around a 0.70 inches (a coastal low tracking more-so inland).\nProbabilities of receiving at least 0.50 inches of precipitation\nranges from 30-50%, with lower probabilities across eastern \nOhio and near I-80 corridor.",
